 pro-choice people in  both parties and in fact during the 1976  election both presidential candidates  tried very hard to distance themselves  from any strong stand on abortion if  anything Republicans were more  pro-choice than Democrats  the main opponents to abortion  immediately after Rome were Catholics  who tended to be Democrat it wasn't at  all inevitable that there would be any  party alignment on abortion much less  the one we have now  the divide was partly engineered by Paul  Warrick a right-wing strategist who in  1979 saw a political opportunity  evangelicals made up 29% of the  population abortion was his ticket to  win their vote many of them had not been  voting at all and had seen politics as  sort of evil corrupt and to some extent  largely irrelevant  Paul Wyrick thought that 'evanchelical  z' could bring elections home for  Republicans in 1980 Republican Ronald  Reagan's presidential campaign  capitalized on abortion despite  previously signing into law the most  liberal abortion bill in the country he  went on to switch his view and call for  abortion to be banned its regard to  abortion there's one individual who's  not being considered at all that's the  one who's being aborted it was a crucial  factor in his win he became the sort of  standard bearer for the idea that the  Republican Party should oppose abortion  this vote running formula has shaped  Republican politics ever since President  Trump too has gone from being pro-choice  I'm very pro-choice to a pro-life  crusade oh the baby is born and then the  doctor and the mother determined whether  or not they will execute the baby I  don't  in the 2016 presidential election mr.  Trump won eighty-one percent of the  evangelical vote president Trump has  used some of the most extreme rhetoric  we've seen on abortion he's gone out of  his way to reassure the anti-abortion  movement the fact that abortion is still  at the heart of party politics in  America means it matters more than ever  to